1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,280
Narrator: Scenario 15. Infrastructure operations.

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:17,472
B: Application response times increased around 02:10 UTC, but CPU and memory remain near their normal levels. Could the database be waiting on storage?

3
00:00:18,072 --> 00:00:28,992
A: Disk latency and queue depth increased at the same time. That supports checking storage contention, although it does not identify which workload is responsible.

4
00:00:29,592 --> 00:00:40,440
C: A backup job started on the same storage pool at 02:00 UTC. Its throughput is higher than usual because it is catching up after a delay.

5
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:50,712
B: The timing seems convincing. Can we stop the backup immediately, or could that leave us without the recovery point required by the business?

6
00:00:51,312 --> 00:01:03,624
C: We should check the backup policy first. The last verified recovery point is still within the required interval, but stopping now would require a documented restart plan.

7
00:01:04,224 --> 00:01:15,432
A: Could we safely reduce backup throughput instead? A controlled reduction would preserve progress and help us observe whether application performance changes with the storage load.

8
00:01:16,032 --> 00:01:27,000
C: Yes. The backup owner has approved a temporary throttle within the runbook. I will apply it and record the time and new throughput limit.

9
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:39,240
B: I will compare transaction completion times before and after the adjustment. Some requests are queued, so the customer experience may recover later than the disk metrics.

10
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:53,184
A: Exactly. We will monitor storage latency, queue drain, and completed business transactions together. Lower disk latency alone will not establish full service recovery.

11
00:01:53,784 --> 00:02:03,720
C: I will also estimate the backup completion time under the throttle and flag any risk to the next required recovery point by 03:00 UTC.

12
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:15,912
B: The application team will check for failed or unusually delayed transactions at that checkpoint. We should not retry completed work just because clients saw timeouts.

13
00:02:16,512 --> 00:02:28,056
A: I will coordinate the 03:00 UTC review and document the evidence. After recovery, we will review backup scheduling and shared pool capacity with both owners.
